#> ─── agentic-codebase template: rule.md.tmpl ──────────────────────────────────────────
#> Emits: .claude/rules/{{RULE_FILE_NAME}}.md   (Claude Code — a NATIVE load path)
#>        Codex: the SAME markdown body, written to <plan-dir>/rules/{{RULE_FILE_NAME}}.md with a
#>        pointer line in the AGENTS.md section — or, for a rule of ~15 lines or fewer, inlined
#>        into that section under `### Conventions` and no file at all.
#>        **The two targets differ here and the difference is not cosmetic.** Claude Code walks
#>        `.claude/rules/` itself (recursively, at Managed / User / Project scope, and at Project
#>        scope for every ancestor of the session cwd), so a rule file dropped into it loads with no
#>        index-doc reference of any kind — the CLAUDE.md pointer is a discoverability nicety, not
#>        the load mechanism (adapters/claude-code.md §4.2, VERIFIED by probe). NOTHING on Codex
#>        walks a prose rules directory, so THERE the rule and its AGENTS.md pointer really are one
#>        artifact and an unreferenced file does nothing (adapters/codex.md §4.2.1). Never tell a
#>        Claude Code user their rule works because the index doc points at it.
#>        **Do not confuse this with `.codex/rules/*.rules`.** Those are a Starlark command-approval
#>        policy — the analogue of Claude Code's permissions allowlist, not of this file — and they
#>        have their own template, `codex-rules.rules.tmpl`. A rule that says "never run npm" is
#>        that artifact; a rule that says "handlers return Result, not exceptions" is this one.
#>
#> Filling rules
#>  1. Replace every placeholder — an ALL_CAPS name in double braces. Delete every "#>" line and
#>     every {{!-- ... --}} block.
#>  2. One rule per file. If you are tempted to write "and also", that is a second rule — write it
#>     as one, with its own scope and its own evidence. There is no limit on how many rules a setup
#>     has; there is a hard limit of one convention per file.
#>  3. Build a rule only from a correction the user made 2+ times, or a convention visible in the
#>     code and confirmed by a count. The evidence line must carry that count.
#>  4. Before writing, check the rule does not contradict another emitted rule on the same key.
#>     verify_artifacts.py fails the build on contradictions.
#>  5. Keep the five agentic-codebase-* lines verbatim; a rerun matches on agentic-codebase-id and rewrites in place.
#>  6. Target length after filling: 20-45 lines. A rule nobody can hold in their head is not a rule.
#> paths — the ONE frontmatter key Claude Code reads on a rule, and the key that decides HOW the
#> rule loads. The literal spelling is `paths`, VERIFIED twice: the two-session probe in
#> adapters/claude-code.md §4.2, and the loader in the 2.1.260 bundle, which reads
#> `frontmatter.paths`, strips a trailing `/**` from each entry, discards an empty list or an
#> all-`**` list, and then matches the survivors gitignore-style against the repo-relative path of
#> the file the model is reading. It reads no other key: an earlier version of this template emitted
#> `scope:`, which Claude Code ignores outright — every rule agentic-codebase built that way loaded EAGERLY,
#> in every session, and the scope it advertised was enforced by nothing. Do not emit `scope:`, and
#> do not emit both "for compatibility": a second spelling only tells a human the rule is scoped
#> when it is not.
#>   * WITH `paths:` — the rule is loaded ON DEMAND, the moment the model touches a matching file,
#>     and costs nothing until then. This is the default: derive the globs from the evidence that
#>     produced the rule (the directories in the corrections, the paths in the commits).
#>   * WITHOUT `paths:` — the rule is loaded EAGERLY at session start, every session, wired or not.
#>     That is real context rent, so adapters/claude-code.md §4.2 caps unscoped rules at 2. Emit one
#>     only when the evidence genuinely spans the whole repo, and DELETE the `paths:` block when you
#>     do — an omitted key and `paths: ["**"]` are the same thing to the loader, and omitting it is
#>     the one a reader cannot misread. `**/*` is NOT the same thing: it survives the all-`**` test,
#>     so it makes the rule lazy-but-matches-everything, which is a third behaviour nobody wants.
#>   * A glob that matches no file in the repo is a SILENT no-op — no error, no warning, nothing in
#>     the transcript. verify_artifacts.py's `rule_scope` is the only thing that will tell you.
#>   * Use repo-relative globs, narrowest set that still covers the evidence, 1-4 entries. A trailing
#>     `/**` is stripped before matching, so `src/api/**` and `src/api` are the same rule.
#>   * On CODEX this key does nothing — nothing there walks a prose rules directory — but it is
#>     harmless, it documents the scope, and the same file serves both targets. What carries the
#>     scope on Codex is the `**Scope:**` prose line below plus the AGENTS.md pointer.
paths:
  - {{PATHS_GLOB_1}}
{{!-- repeat/trim: one line per glob, 1-4 entries. For a genuinely repo-wide rule, delete the
     `paths:` key and both entries entirely rather than writing `**` — and remember it then counts
     against the context budget of 2 unscoped rules: a constraint on this one rule *form*, because
     an unscoped rule is carried in every session's context, not a limit on how many rules the run
     produces. Never describe it to the user as a cap. --}}
  - {{PATHS_GLOB_2}}
agentic-codebase-id: {{AGENTIC_CODEBASE_ID}}
agentic-codebase-version: 1
agentic-codebase-generated: {{GENERATED_DATE}}
agentic-codebase-evidence: {{EVIDENCE_LINE}}
# Safe to delete or edit. This work was brought to you by Agentic Studio.
---

# {{RULE_TITLE}}

#> Repeat the scope in prose, always, and keep it identical to the `paths:` globs above. The key
#> controls LOADING; this line tells the model and the human what the rule governs once it is
#> loaded, and it is the only scope that survives being read on Codex, quoted into a review, or
#> pasted into another tool. For a repo-wide rule with no `paths:` key, write `**Scope:** the whole
#> repository.` and say so plainly.
**Scope:** {{SCOPE_PROSE}}

